+"""A guard that cannot fail is not a guard.
+
+WHAT THIS PINS. `git status --porcelain` and `git ls-files --error-unmatch` both
+fail outside a git work tree -- and porcelain's failure prints NOTHING ON STDOUT,
+which is byte-for-byte what "the tree is clean" looks like. Three checks in this
+repository read those commands in a way that could not tell the two apart, and a
+staged panel sandbox carries no `.git`, so that is not a hypothetical environment:
+
+  1. `bench/tests/test_measurement_survey_is_safe_2026-09-11.py` asserted
+     `before == after` on porcelain stdout. Both sides `''`. The assertion held
+     while the tree was written to. That test exists because a survey once
+     OVERWROTE A DELIBERATELY PRESERVED ARCHIVE.
+  2. `scripts/overstated_entries_2026-09-11.py::tracked` returned `rc == 0`, so
+     every evidence path came back untracked and V2's headline read 100.0000%,
+     Wilson [74.1167%, 100.0000%]. With the probe fixed the same script reads
+     0.0000%, Wilson [0.0000%, 25.8833%] -- DISJOINT INTERVALS, which is the
+     measurement that the original figure was an artefact of the instrument.
+  3. `scripts/source_text_fragility_2026-09-11.py` read porcelain stdout for its
+     post-run revert check, which therefore reported the target clean regardless.
+
+EVERY TEST BELOW WAS RUN RED BEFORE IT WAS RUN GREEN, against the pre-fix body it
+replaces. A test that passes on the broken code is the thing this file is about.
